# The two JSON databases live in S3 (see backend/store.mjs). npm run db:pull
# brings them back locally when a seed or ingest needs to rewrite them.
# backend/db.json
# backend/settings.json
# One db.json per dataset (db.CAPEX.json, ...) - same reason, same place: S3 holds them.
# backend/db.*.json
# The report prototype's dataset, which now lives in the bucket beside the two databases.
# backend/reports_prototype.json

# Local credentials - never commit. Covered by *.local above; named here so a
# stray copy (.backup) cannot slip past it.
#
# These three lines were commented out once and the keys were committed twice as a result, both times
# blocked by GitHub push protection. They are not a preference. A key in a tracked file is scraped off
# GitHub by bots within minutes of a push, and `check-docs` asserts no tracked file matches AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}
# for the same reason. If a credential needs to travel, it goes in the deploy environment, never here.
backend/.env.local
backend/.env.local.backup
# The deployed box's S3 configuration and its signing credentials.
#
# Ignored for the same reason .env.local is, and it still reaches AWS: .ebignore replaces
# .gitignore for bundling, so a file git never sees is put in the eb deploy zip anyway. That is
# the whole mechanism - the values are hardcoded so the deployed process needs no eb setenv, and
# the access key still never lands on GitHub. Do not add it to backend/.ebignore.
backend/.ebextensions/02-credentials.config

# The Elastic Beanstalk source bundle, which is itself a credential.
#
# npm run bundle:eb ships .ebextensions/02-credentials.config, so the zip carries the AWS access key.
# It is a build artefact either way, but this is the reason it must never be committed - and the reason
# not to mail it, attach it to a ticket, or drop it in a bucket other people can read.
backend-eb.zip

# The built SPA, zipped for hand-off to S3 + CloudFront.
#
# A build artefact and nothing else - it holds no credential, because every VITE_* value is inlined
# at build time and none of them can hold a secret. Ignored so a 2.4 MB copy of dist/ cannot be
# committed beside the source that produced it; rebuild it rather than keeping one around.
frontend-dist.zip
